# SASS of the *production* entry points, both trees, with source-line attribution.
#
# The review of #26 found that the loop explanation rested on `sass-plane-loop.log`, which is
# `plane_reduce_body<MaxFold, float>` compiled alone in a probe kernel called `probe_max`. This
# file takes the same claim on the global entry points themselves.
#
# $ nvcc -std=c++17 -cubin -arch=sm_80 -lineinfo -I cuda/include -o reduce.cubin cuda/src/reduce.cu
# $ nvcc -std=c++17 -cubin -arch=sm_80 -lineinfo -I cuda/include -o elementwise.cubin cuda/src/elementwise.cu
# $ nvdisasm -g -c -sf <cubin>        # -g adds the source line of the instructions it has one for
# CUDA 12.6 (Build cuda_12.6.r12.6/compiler.35059454_0). The base side is the same commands in a
# worktree of 3c8d82c. Which compiler built the 2026-11-08 timing binaries was not recorded, so
# this is code generation from the source, not the disassembly of the timed binaries.
#
# Taken on evo2 (Ryzen AI MAX+ 395, no NVIDIA device, no driver): compiling and disassembling
# need no GPU, so nothing in this file measures anything.
#
# Reading: each excerpt starts with the `.section`/`.sectioninfo`/`.text.<entry>` header nvdisasm
# prints for the enclosing section, then the section's address range. The reduction excerpts are
# one complete iteration of the fold loop: its head label, its body, and the branch back to the
# head. The `t4a_cuda_binary` excerpts are the arm the entry dispatches to at 0x12b0, including
# the labels its NaN tests skip to (`.L_x_273` / `.L_x_275`); the arms' join, `.L_x_267`, belongs
# to the dispatch further down the entry and is outside the excerpt. Only the trailing hex
# encoding and the `//## File` path prefix are stripped; no instruction is removed.
#
# abi.cuh per tree, definition line to closing brace, this branch -> 3c8d82c:
#   t4a_cuda_nan_max(float)   748-752 -> 744-752
#   t4a_cuda_nan_max(double)  754-758 -> 754-762
#   t4a_cuda_nan_min(float)   760-764 -> 764-772
#   t4a_cuda_nan_min(double)  766-770 -> 774-782

